LLMNR = Link-Local Multicast Name Resolution.

It works like this: when a computer cannot find a name in DNS, it asks the local network: “Does anyone know the address of ‘printer’, ‘server’, or ‘WPAD’?”

Problem: a malicious computer on the same network may respond first and impersonate the searched name.

Disabling LLMNR:

Open regedit.exe

HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows NT\DNSClient

Inside the folder DNSClient right-click in the right pane, select New -> DWORD (32-bit) Value. Name it

EnableMulticast

and set the value to 0 (disabled) 1 = enabled Then restart the computer.

---------------------------------------------------------------

WPAD = Web Proxy Auto-Discovery.

Used for automatic proxy detection on the network. The computer may search for, e.g.:

wpad
wpad.domain.local

Problem: someone on the network may impersonate WPAD and provide a fake proxy through which some traffic will pass.

Disabling WPAD:

Open: regedit.exe

HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings\WinHttp

Inside the WinHttp folder, right-click in the right pane, select New:

DWORD (32-bit) Value. Name:

DisableWpad

and set its value to 1 (value 0 enables it)

HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings

Inside the folder Internet Settings right-click in the right pane, select New: DWORD (32-bit) Value. Name it

AutoDetect

and set the value to 0 (value 1 enables it)

NOTE: Changes to DNS / LLMNR / WPAD may affect:

Active Directory, older NAS devices, network printers, VPN solutions, corporate environments.




